ersonal_status overlaps conceptually with other demographic features like sex and marital status. It introduces redundancy without adding significant value.\ndf.drop(columns=['personal_status'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column own_telephone is dropped\n# The feature own_telephone is outdated and unlikely to be a strong indicator of financial behavior. It is redundant in the context of modern financial datasets.\ndf.drop(columns=['own_telephone'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column existing_credits is dropped\n# The column existing_credits has low variability (range = [1, 4]). Its minimal variation limits its predictive usefulness, especially when compared to other richer financial features.\ndf.drop(columns=['existing_credits'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here is the response rephrased and formatted as per your request. The solution aims to include three well-designed feature engineering steps while retaining simplicity, clarity, and utility. It avoids the mistakes previously made:\n\n### Possible Next Steps:\n\npython\n# Feature name: duration_to_age_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of loan duration to the applicant's age, representing the relative loan term length compared to their life stage.\n# Usefulness: A higher ratio may indicate financial strain or risk for individuals with longer loan terms relative to their age, helping capture default likelihood.\ndf['duration_to_age_ratio'] = df['duration'] / (df['age'] + 1e-9)  # Prevents division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: credit_amount_log\n# Feature description: Compute the logarithm of the credit_amount to normalize its distribution and reduce skewness.\n# Usefulness: Many financial features like credit amounts are highly skewed. Taking the log helps normalize the data, improving model interpretability and performance.\ndf['credit_amount_log'] = df['credit_amount'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))  # Added 1 to avoid log(0)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: 